Codex Usage Tray Lite {{VERSION}}
================================

Portable Windows x64 package. This is an unofficial community derivative of
saveway/codex-usage-monitor, based on {{BASELINE_TAG}} ({{BASELINE_COMMIT}}).
License: GPL-3.0-only. See LICENSE.txt and THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md.

Run
---
Extract the entire ZIP to one folder, then start CodexUsageTrayLite.exe.
Keep the runtimes, ja-JP, ko-KR, zh-CN, and zh-TW subfolders and all DLL/config
files beside the EXE. The four language subfolders contain the compiled Japanese,
Korean, and Chinese resources.
See RELEASE_NOTES.md and CHANGELOG.md for the English release documentation.
For a complete Simplified Chinese package guide, start with README.zh-CN.txt.
The Simplified Chinese release documents are RELEASE_NOTES.zh-CN.md and
CHANGELOG.zh-CN.md.

Usage source
------------
The tray menu offers Usage source > WebView2 or Codex CLI. WebView2 is the
default. Codex CLI mode requires an existing Codex CLI installation that is
already signed in with ChatGPT (run "codex login" if needed). The CLI itself,
Node.js, and its credentials are not included or read by this package.

Switching to either source performs one immediate validation fetch. Returning
to WebView2 reuses its saved login profile; the login window is needed only if
that validation reports login-required. A CLI app-server process is used only
while reading usage and is then closed. Refresh now can retry a missing or
unsigned-in CLI; periodic refresh starts only after a successful read. Proxy
settings and Clear login session apply only to WebView2. The Help item above
About provides a short in-app summary of both source modes and the application's
security and privacy boundaries.

Refresh interval
----------------
Refresh interval offers 1, 2, 5, 10, 15, 30, and 60 minutes. The default
remains 15 minutes; the shorter intervals are explicit opt-in choices.

Icon style
----------
Icon style, directly below Refresh interval, offers 5-Hour Only, Weekly Only,
Stacked (Default), and Side by Side. The single checked choice is saved and
restored; existing choices are preserved and new settings still default to
Stacked. Stacked keeps the established overlay icon. Side by Side uses a gapless Weekly-left
and 5-Hour-right pair of flat rectangles when both limits apply; when 5-Hour is
authoritatively not applicable, Weekly expands across the complete inner frame.
Ambiguous applicability remains Unknown. Weekly Only hides the 5-Hour layer.
5-Hour Only hides Weekly; confirmed Pro Weekly-only data shows an empty frame
with "i" instead of inventing a quota value. Unknown, error, and login-required
states remain distinct. The boxed "i", "?", and "L" use large bold red glyphs
uniformly fitted without changing their original aspect ratios; the error slash
keeps its separate existing treatment.

After a successful refresh, the tooltip contains quota information only: a
5-Hour line plus Weekly for an applicable dual-window account, including Plus,
or Weekly alone for a detected Pro or authoritative Weekly-only account. Email and source
tags are not added to the tooltip. The tray status rows are Email / User Level,
5-Hour, Weekly, Usage resets, and Last updated. The 5-Hour menu row remains
visible as N/A for Pro and Weekly-only accounts.

In 5-Hour Only mode, the tooltip reports only 5-Hour and shows `5-Hour N/A`
when that quota is confirmed unavailable. Weekly Only and Both retain the
existing tooltip rules above rather than filtering text to the visible layers.

CLI reads Email and supported exact Free/Go/Plus/Pro levels from the documented
account/read response. WebView2 uses a small same-origin session request and an
exact rendered-page badge fallback for the level; if that optional lookup is
unavailable, usage refresh still succeeds and User Level stays Unknown. Pro
5x/20x variants are intentionally not detected in this version.

The Usage resets row shows available banked resets when the selected source
returns a count. CLI reads it from the same rate-limit response.
WebView2 accepts an explicit numeric statement, or uses a normal desktop-layout
viewport and a short delayed DOM probe to count enabled "Use reset" actions in
the rendered "Usage limit resets" section (one action row equals one reset).
Descriptive, disabled, hidden, and out-of-section text is not counted. Missing
or stale-source data is shown as Unknown, not zero. An explicit page banner
stating that no usage-limit resets are available maps to 0. This value is not
added to the tooltip. Background WebView2 creates an unshown, non-activating host HWND,
so periodic refresh does not take foreground activation or interrupt the
Windows screen saver.

Appearance
----------
Use Theme > Follow Windows, Light, or Dark from the tray menu. The selection
applies to the tray menu, Proxy, Help, About, the login-window chrome, and WebView2's
preferred page color scheme. Individual login providers may keep their own
website theme.

Use Language > English, Simplified Chinese, Traditional Chinese, Japanese, or
Korean to change the application UI immediately. A fresh installation selects
the matching Chinese, Japanese, or Korean language from the Windows UI language
when applicable and otherwise starts in English. Existing installations keep
their saved language until changed. Tooltips intentionally retain their compact
English quota format.

The EXE and File Explorer use the Light Option C code-meter identity icon by
default. Proxy, Help, About, and Login / Usage title bars choose the matching
Light or Dark icon from the same effective palette as the window. Explicit
application Light/Dark modes therefore stay visually consistent; Follow Windows
resolves both the window and icon from the current Windows app theme. Open Follow
Windows dialogs refresh after Windows appearance changes. The live tray icon
remains the dynamic dual-quota meter. Application prompts use localized button
captions while retaining the familiar Windows status glyphs and modal behavior.

Target requirements
-------------------
- Windows 11 x64
- .NET Framework 4.8 or later
- Microsoft Edge WebView2 Evergreen Runtime (for WebView2 source)
- Installed Codex CLI on PATH and "codex login" (for Codex CLI source)

Windows 11 normally provides .NET Framework 4.8 or later, and many Windows 11
installations already have WebView2 Runtime. These two Microsoft system
components are not embedded in this small portable ZIP. Codex CLI is likewise
not bundled. Missing prerequisites are reported instead of being silently
installed.

Local data
----------
Runtime data is created only under:
  %LOCALAPPDATA%\CodexUsageTrayLite

The full account email is held in process memory only and is never written to
settings or logs. Logs use structured level=info|warning|error and event fields;
successful identity reads record only a fixed masked address. Open
logs also records process-lifecycle diagnostics without quota values. Codex CLI
entries show whether stdin shutdown was graceful or whether
the exact launched process required the timed Kill/Job fallback. WebView2
entries separately show controller Close, unexpected process failure details,
and the runtime's actual BrowserProcessExited event; this app never kills a
WebView2 process.

After a normal Codex CLI root exit, descendants receive up to 500 ms to exit
naturally before Job Object cleanup is classified. Open logs launches the log
file directly through its Windows file association; Open config folder still
opens the application data directory.

CodexUsageTrayLite.log is the active log for the current local day. Before the
first write after local midnight, the prior non-empty log is archived as
CodexUsageTrayLite_YYYYMMDD.log. Daily archives stay in the logs directory and
are not removed automatically.

The webview2-profile folder contains sensitive authenticated session data.
Never upload, share, or publicly synchronize it. This package contains no
profile, settings, log, token, cookie, password, dump, PDB, or environment file.

Validation
----------
The packaging run completed {{TEST_COUNT}} automated tests before creating this
ZIP. SHA256SUMS.txt covers every application file inside the package. The
matching .zip.sha256 file beside the ZIP covers the ZIP itself.

The documented 8-12 hour naturally spaced WebView2 soak remains a release gate
before public distribution. This build has no desktop Widget, Windows
toast/balloon notification, telemetry, auto-update, installer, cloud backend,
or credential extraction.

Corresponding source: https://github.com/zjwww/Codex-Usage-Tray-Lite/tree/v{{VERSION}}
